@baseUrl = http://localhost:8080
@apiKey = <replace-with-generated-token>

### 1. Health Check (always 200 OK)
GET {{baseUrl}}/health
Accept: application/json

### 2. Ping Check (always 200 OK)
GET {{baseUrl}}/ping
Accept: application/json

### 3. Create API Key Token (custom TTL)
# @name createToken
POST {{baseUrl}}/api-key
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "time_window": 5,
  "max_requests": 3,
  "blocked_duration": 10,
  "token_ttl": 3600
}

### 4. Create API Key Token (default TTL when token_ttl is 0)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api-key
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "time_window": 5,
  "max_requests": 2,
  "blocked_duration": 5,
  "token_ttl": 0
}

### 5. Create API Key Token (invalid parameters -> 400 Bad Request)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api-key
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "time_window": 0,
  "max_requests": 2,
  "blocked_duration": 5,
  "token_ttl": 10
}

### 6. Protected Endpoint - IP Rate Limit (no token)
# Default config: 2 requests per 5s window; 3rd request returns 429 Too Many Requests + Retry-After
GET {{baseUrl}}/protected
Accept: application/json

### 7. Protected Endpoint - Token Rate Limit (token overrides IP limit)
# Uses @apiKey (or {{createToken.response.body.token}} in VS Code REST Client)
GET {{baseUrl}}/protected
Accept: application/json
API_KEY: {{apiKey}}

### 8. Protected Endpoint - Unknown / Expired Token (falls back to IP limit)
GET {{baseUrl}}/protected
Accept: application/json
API_KEY: non-existent-token

### 9. Protected Endpoint - Redis Outage Simulation
# Stop Redis (e.g., `docker compose stop redis`) and send this request:
# - With FAIL_OPEN=false (default): returns 503 Service Unavailable
# - With FAIL_OPEN=true: returns 200 OK (bypass with structured WARN log)
GET {{baseUrl}}/protected
Accept: application/json
